        "Medium": [
          {"id":"amz_ds_m1","year":"Amazon SDE-1 2024","q":"Two numbers in an array summing to target. Most efficient approach?","opts":["O(n²) nested loop","Sort + two pointer O(n log n)","HashMap single pass O(n)","Binary search O(n log n)"],"ans":2,"exp":"HashMap: for each x, check if (target-x) exists. O(n) time, O(n) space. Classic Two Sum (LeetCode #1). Amazon's most frequently asked question type."},
          {"id":"amz_ds_m2","year":"Amazon SDE-1 2023","q":"Which data structure gives O(1) average for insert, delete, search?","opts":["Array","BST","HashMap","Linked List"],"ans":2,"exp":"HashMap: O(1) average for all 3 operations (with good hash function). BST gives O(log n). Worst case HashMap is O(n) due to collisions — Java 8+ uses trees in buckets."},
          {"id":"amz_ds_m3","year":"Amazon Intern 2023","q":"Find the middle element of a linked list in one pass. Best approach?","opts":["Count nodes then traverse again","Two pointers: slow and fast","Sort then pick middle","Use array conversion"],"ans":1,"exp":"Slow-fast pointer (Floyd's): slow moves 1 step, fast moves 2. When fast reaches end, slow is at middle. O(n) time, O(1) space — one pass only."},
          {"id":"amz_ds_m4","year":"Amazon SDE-1 2024","q":"HashMap internal structure and worst-case get() complexity?","opts":["Array O(1) always","Linked list O(n) always","Array of buckets/lists O(n) worst","Tree O(log n) always"],"ans":2,"exp":"HashMap = array of buckets, each a linked list. Average O(1). Worst O(n) with all collisions. Java 8+ converts to red-black tree when bucket size > 8, giving O(log n) worst case."},
          {"id":"amz_ds_m5","year":"Amazon SDE-1 2023","q":"Which traversal visits nodes in sorted order in a BST?","opts":["Preorder","Postorder","Inorder","Level order"],"ans":2,"exp":"Inorder traversal (Left → Root → Right) of a BST visits nodes in ascending sorted order. This property makes BSTs powerful for ordered data operations."}
